 ("h2", "Classification of Species (IUCN)"),
 ("table", ["Category", "Meaning", "Examples"], [["Normal species", "Population levels are normal for survival", "Cattle, sal, pine, rodents"], ["Endangered species", "In danger of extinction; survival is difficult if the factors causing decline continue", "Black buck, crocodile, Indian wild ass, Indian rhino, lion-tailed macaque, sangai (brow-antlered deer)"], ["Vulnerable species", "Population declining; likely to move into the endangered category", "Blue sheep, Asiatic elephant, Gangetic dolphin"], ["Rare species", "Small population, may move into endangered or vulnerable", "Himalayan brown bear, wild Asiatic buffalo, desert fox, hornbill"], ["Endemic species", "Found only in some areas, usually isolated by natural barriers", "Andaman teal, Nicobar pigeon, Andaman wild pig, mithun in Arunachal Pradesh"], ["Extinct species", "No longer found anywhere", "Asiatic cheetah, pink-headed duck"]]),
 ("p", "The IUCN is the International Union for Conservation of Nature and Natural Resources; its Red Data Book keeps a record of endangered species."),
 ("h2", "Why Are Forest and Wildlife Resources Declining?"),
 ("ul", ["**Colonial expansion:** railways, agriculture, commercial and scientific forestry and mining; large areas of forest were cleared after 1951 for river valley projects, agriculture and plantations. Between 1951 and 1980, over 26,200 sq km of forest area was converted into agricultural land.", "**Shifting cultivation** (‘slash and burn’) in the north-east and parts of central India.", "**Large development projects**: the Narmada Sagar and others submerge forests; **mining**, such as in West Bengal's Buxa Tiger Reserve, threatens habitats.", "**Grazing and fuelwood collection**, **poaching and illegal trade** in skins, bones and horns, and **forest fires**.", "**Environmental destruction is not just a result of the poor** but of the lifestyles of the rich who consume more; the unequal share of resources leads to depletion."]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Habitat destruction, hunting, over-exploitation, environmental pollution, poisoning and forest fires** are the main factors in biodiversity loss. Loss of species also reduces the health of the ecosystem and affects human well-being, especially of the forest-dwelling poor and women."),
 ("h2", "Conservation of Forest and Wildlife"),
 ("ul", ["Conservation preserves the ecological diversity (water, air, soil) and our life support systems, and genetic diversity of plants and animals for better growth of species and breeds.", "The **Indian Wildlife (Protection) Act of 1972** provided for an all India list of protected species, banned hunting, protected habitats and curbed trade; many national parks and wildlife sanctuaries were set up.", "**Project Tiger (1973)** was launched to protect the tiger; the tiger population had dropped sharply from 55,000 in the early twentieth century to 1,827 in 1973. Tiger reserves include Corbett (Uttarakhand), Sunderbans (West Bengal), Bandhavgarh (Madhya Pradesh), Sariska (Rajasthan), Manas (Assam) and Periyar (Kerala).", "Other species have schemes of protection, such as the one-horned rhino in Assam and Bengal, and the Asiatic lion in the Gir forest of Gujarat."]),
 ("h3", "Forest categories (as per the Indian Forest Policy)"),
 ("table", ["Type", "Features"], [["Reserved forests", "Most valuable for conservation; more than half of the total forest land; no grazing or hunting without permission"], ["Protected forests", "Nearly one-third; protected from further depletion; livelihood use is allowed"], ["Unclassed forests", "Other forests and wastelands belonging to government and private individuals and communities"]]),
 ("p", "Reserved and protected forests are also called **permanent forest estates**, maintained for timber and other forest produce. **Madhya Pradesh** has the largest area under permanent forests."),
 ("h2", "Community and Conservation"),
 ("ul", ["In many parts of India, local communities protect forests because their livelihoods depend on them: villages in Sariska's **Alwar district (Rajasthan)** fought against mining and declared about 1,200 hectares of forest as **Bhairodev Dakav ‘Sonchuri’** and made their own rules to protect wildlife.", "**Sacred groves** are patches of forest preserved by tradition with beliefs of deities; trees and animals in them are protected.", "The **Chipko movement** in the Himalayas (Tehri-Garhwal) resisted deforestation; the **Beej Bachao Andolan** in Tehri and **Navdanya** showed that diverse crops can be grown without chemicals.", "**Joint Forest Management (JFM)** began in **1988** when Odisha passed the first resolution; local communities work with the forest department to manage and restore degraded forests and get a share of the produce.", "Religious beliefs protect species such as the **peepal and tulsi**, **black buck** among the Bishnoi people of Rajasthan, and the **Himalayan yew** (Taxus wallichiana), a medicinal plant, found in Himachal and Arunachal Pradesh, which yields taxol for cancer treatment and is endangered by over-harvesting."]),
